(vector (map (:db/id -1),
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608196100"),
  (:adr/title "Thirty-four verifiers, and what runs them"),
  (:adr/status "accepted"),
  (:adr/date "2026-08-19"),
  (:adr/body
  "## The question ADR-2608196000 left\n\nThat ADR installed the scheduler for the fifteen registered detectors and ended\nby naming what it had not measured: there are 34 `verify-*.cljs` in `scripts/`,\n15 are registered detectors and 5 are named by a fleet gate or a hook. **What\nruns the other fourteen was not measured.**\n\nMeasured now, by matching each script name against the detector registry,\n`scripts/fleet-ci/gates.edn`, `.claude/hooks/` and `scripts/tasks.edn`:\n\n  - 19 have a runner\n  - **15 have none**\n\nOf the fifteen, three have a *twin* under `scripts/fleet-ci/gates/` with the\nsame stem -- `capital-pools`, `kotobase-persistence-policy`, `source-nul-bytes`.\nThose checks do run; what is unrun is the root copy. **Two implementations of\none check is the drift `verify-vendored-copies` exists to catch**, so the twins\nare a finding of their own rather than a reassurance.\n\nThat leaves **twelve with no runner and no twin.**\n\n## What they are, by what they read\n\n  - reads `orgs/` -- app-content-address, d1-kotobase-migration,\n    duplicate-registrations, kip-quorum, kotoba-pages, source-path-pins.\n    These cannot be fleet gates for the structural reason ADR-2608124800 gives,\n    so their home is the detector registry, which now has a scheduler.\n  - root-only -- adr-edn-shape, artifact-path-policy, clj-everywhere,\n    no-babashka, shadow-warnings-as-errors.\n  - needs the network -- endpoint-health, kotobase-persistence-policy.\n\n## Ran, corrected\n\n    verify-artifact-path-policy     exit 0    checked=27 ok=1 skipped=26\n    verify-capital-pools            exit 0    pool 1 / tranche 4, clean\n    verify-clj-everywhere           exit 1    41 lines\n    verify-shadow-warnings-as-errors exit 1   172 findings\n    verify-source-nul-bytes         exit 1    59s\n    verify-no-babashka              exit 1    4853 findings, 154s\n    verify-adr-edn-shape            exit 1    takes <file.edn>, not a directory\n\n`verify-adr-edn-shape` is not an entry point at all: it checks one file and\nprints a usage line. It is a helper whose caller is missing, which is a\ndifferent thing from an entry point whose schedule is missing, and the census\nshould not treat them alike.\n\n## Registered: nothing\n\nThe two that exit 0 are the only registration candidates, and neither should be\nregistered today.\n\n`verify-capital-pools` already has a gate twin. Registering the root copy would\nmake a third implementation of one check.\n\n`verify-artifact-path-policy` skips 26 of the 27 things it checks and reports\n`ok=1`. On a fleet node, which gets only the root tree, it would skip more --\nplausibly all of them -- and **a gate that skips everything and exits 0 is a\ngreen light for an unmeasured property.** It needs an evidence floor before it\ncan be a gate, which is per-script work and not a registry line.\n\n**Measuring and not acting is the outcome here.** The alternative was five\ngates.edn lines that would have looked like progress.\n\n## Two measurement errors, both mine, both caught\n\nFirst: exit codes were collected as `out=$(cmd | tail -1); code=$?`, which\ncaptures `tail`'s status, not the script's. That reported **exit 0 for all\nseven**, including two that exit 1. Re-run without the pipe, the table above is\nwhat they actually do. A measurement that answers about the wrong process is\nthe defect this workspace keeps finding, and it produced a clean-looking result.\n\nSecond: `nbb --classpath \".:scripts/nbb_compat\" scripts/verify-adr-edn-shape.cljs`\nmade the script read `.:scripts/nbb_compat` as its input file -- the argument\ntrap CLAUDE.md documents (`<dir>` は引数の先頭に置く). Passing the directory\nafter the script did not help, because the classpath value is still first among\nnon-flag arguments.\n\n## Next\n\n  - Register the six `orgs/`-reading verifiers in `manifest/orgs-detectors.edn`,\n    one at a time, each with a measured interval and timeout rather than a\n    guessed one. The registry demands that, and bulk registration would defeat\n    it.\n  - Decide per twin whether the root copy or the gate copy is the survivor.\n  - Give `verify-artifact-path-policy` an evidence floor, then gate it.\n\n## 2026-08-19 — the scheduler is installed, and a full pass is 14 minutes\n\n`~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick.plist` is loaded. Verified\nby `launchctl kickstart`, not by `launchctl list`: a job can be listed and\nstill fail to execute, and a scheduler that cannot start looks exactly like a\nscheduler with nothing to do. It ran, wrote to its own log, and exited 0.\n\n**A full pass is 14m10s** (05:35:24Z to 05:49:34Z, 17 detectors run, 2 not\ndue). Before measuring I compared the sum of the registry's `:timeout-ms`\nagainst `StartInterval` and worried that 402 minutes would not fit in 360.\nThat was the wrong number: a timeout is a ceiling, not a duration. The longest\ndetector took 232s against a 90-minute allowance. Six hours is ample.\n\nThe eleven detectors that had never run were added between 08-13 and 08-19 and\nwere each executed by hand at the moment they were added — which is why they\nhad no state. There was no hidden blocker; `admit!` passes all nineteen. State\nnow holds 19 detectors, none never-run.\n\n**Two of the nineteen refuse to answer, and both are right to.**\n\n- `verify-kip-quorum` exits 3 with `SCANNED 2`: its self-check passes, policy\n  reads 2-of-3, and neither KIP in the tree requires a quorum. It has no\n  verdict to give, and says so rather than reporting the same clean as two\n  KIPs with valid quorums.\n- `verify-valueflows-binding` exited 2 with five `high` findings, and all five\n  were artefacts of the sparse cone — see the fix below.\n\n### A detector reported on files it could not see\n\nAll five of that detector's inputs live under `90-docs/valueflows`, which is in\nnobody's default cone. `fs.existsSync` answered false for each, and the checks\nconcluded three projections were stale and the pinned price observations\nabsent. Every one of the five is in `origin/main` and marked `S`. The findings\nwere about the checkout, not the workspace, and this superproject is always\nsparse — so this was the normal case, not an unlucky one.\n\nAdmission now asks git before any check runs: for an input missing from disk,\nif HEAD has it, print `SCANNED 0`, emit one\n`cannot-answer-sparse-checkout` finding, exit 2. An input absent from HEAD too\nstill reaches the original checks, so a real finding is not swallowed.\n\nMeasured both ways: sparse, exit 2 and one finding where there were five; the\nsame tree with the cone extended, exit 1, `SCANNED 1398`, and **one** finding —\n`binding-stale`, which is real and had been buried under four artefacts.\n\nSwept every detector for path literals present in HEAD but absent from disk:\none affected, now fixed. The sweep reads string literals, so a path assembled\nat runtime would not appear in it.\n\n### Open\n\n- `binding-stale` is unfixed: the committed\n  `90-docs/valueflows/itonami-business-vf.datoms.edn` disagrees with the\n  workspace. Regenerating needs a checkout with both the cone and `orgs/`.\n- The eleven first runs are baselines. Their counts are large (855 for\n  `verify-jvm-dependency-surface`, 281 for `verify-appview-page-summary`) and\n  mean nothing yet; the next pass is the first that can say NEW or RESOLVED.\n\n\n### A detector whose inputs are outside the cone is permanently inconclusive\n\nRefusing to answer was the right fix, but on this machine the tick runs in the\nsparse main checkout, so `verify-valueflows-binding` would have refused on\nevery pass forever. Honest and useless are not far apart: this file's own\nstandard is that a gate which never goes green is as empty as one that never\ngoes red, because nobody can act on either.\n\nSo `90-docs/valueflows` (6 files, 1.1 MB) is now in the main checkout's cone,\nand the detector answers there: `SCANNED 1399`, 0 findings, exit 0.\n\n**The general rule: a detector registered in `manifest/orgs-detectors.edn`\nneeds its inputs inside the cone of the checkout the tick runs in, or it is\nnot a detector on this machine.** The registry does not declare inputs today,\nso nothing checks this — the next one added with out-of-cone inputs will be\nfound the way this one was, by reading a refusal.\n\nThe report layer already handles it correctly: the session-start hook prints\n`直近の run は inconclusive —— 結果は使えない` from `:last-status`, so an\ninconclusive detector is never rendered as a clean one. The tick also keeps\nthe previous finding set on a non-ok run, so a detector that fails to start\ncannot report standing findings as RESOLVED.\n\n`binding-stale` is closed: `bound` went 1398 to 1399 because\n`orgs/cloud-itonami/keihi` (経費, expense reimbursement) exists and the\nprojection predated it. Regenerated with the generator, `cbad8ed14b4`.\n")))
